    "created_at": "2022-08-23T17:07:56Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-08-23T17:07:56Z",
    "body": "Fun historical detail: conic-gradient() inherited its angle behavior from linear-gradient(), which was *originally* specified to have 0deg to the right. It still wouldn't have matched canvas, tho, as it specified that increasing angles go CCW (aka how angles usually work in math), but in canvas increasing angles to CW. (In both cases, it swings from 0deg at the +x axis to 90deg at the +y axis, it's just that math and computer graphics usually have their Y axises flipped.)\r\n\r\n(We changed linear-gradient() to be consistent with other angle usages in CSS, which were all either bearing angles (0deg N, 90deg E), or directionless \"rotation\" angles (like `rotate()`) that nevertheless were consistently explained in tutorials using bearing angles.)\r\n\r\nAnyway yeah canvas should be consistent across its methods, so this was good to close. :+1: ",
